1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
They're in every skyscraper we erect and every road we pave.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Rocks are the beginning of everything.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
The indispensable ingredients of the mundane

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and the monumental.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
It's some of the finest marble in the world.

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Some can even float,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
while others can supply us with unlimited power.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:28,000
This is renewable energy that works around the clock.

9
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:32,000
billions of years old or as young as this morning,

10
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:35,000
they hold the secrets of the universe.

11
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:36,000
Fire.

12
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:40,000
Now, Runks on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Runks.

14
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:59,140
They may be the most underappreciated objects

15
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:03,700
in the natural world, but we'd be stone cold out of luck

16
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,340
without them.

17
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,740
Besides providing us with shelter,

18
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:12,840
we extract metal from rocks to construct our machines.

19
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,160
Whether you're sitting in a chair made of steel

20
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:16,660
or you're driving a car made of steel,

21
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:20,060
that steel came from rocks.

22
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:22,700
We take heat from them for warmth

23
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,000
and precious minerals to make medicine.

24
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:31,400
We rely on rocks to make soil to grow plants.

25
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:34,740
At one time, we used them for weapons.

26
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:38,640
They season our food and add sparkle and wealth

27
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:40,480
to our lives.

28
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,080
If you're operating a computer,

29
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:44,480
the silicon chips that make up an important part of that

30
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:47,120
computer come from rocks.

31
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:51,700
The Earth is one huge ball of rock 25,000 miles around

32
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:56,000
and over 4.5 billion years old.

33
00:01:56,001 --> 00:02:01,640
But a question, what are the most valuable rocks on Earth?

34
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,240
They very well may be NASA's collection of lunar rocks,

35
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:09,640
located at the Lyndon Johnson Space Center near Houston.

36
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:11,780
They're priceless, because we can't bring any more back

37
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:14,180
until we have a new system to get back to the moon.

38
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,860
So there's no way to put a price on them.

39
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:19,360
They're housed in a special building

40
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:22,100
at the center, which was constructed to quarantine

41
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,200
astronauts and material brought back from the moon during the

42
00:02:25,201 --> 00:02:27,100
missions.

43
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,300
The building where we keep the rocks was specially designed

44
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,700
to hold them.

45
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:36,180
It can withstand a Category 5 hurricane or a tornado

46
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:41,280
or almost any other natural event that you could think of.

47
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:44,080
The lab is off limits to the general public,

48
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:46,720
and those who work here must observe stringent cleanliness

49
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,620
protocols to protect the rocks from any form of earth

50
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:50,620
contamination.

51
00:02:50,621 --> 00:02:54,220
contamination.

52
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:56,600
All workers who come in contact with the rocks

53
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,400
must wear a bunny suit.

54
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:02,240
The suit that I'm wearing is a nylon clean room suit.

55
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:04,860
The air that comes into this lab is filtered very well with

56
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:08,740
very, very small HEPA filters, so the air stays very clean.

57
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:10,840
Well, this is the door to the vault where we keep our lunar

58
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:12,600
samples safe.

59
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,440
This is equivalent to a Federal Reserve Bank vault,

60
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:19,240
and it's a very, very secure kind of storage.

61
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:23,840
This is a very substantial door, as you will see.

62
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:26,620
And inside here, we keep the samples that are still

63
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:27,720
pristine.

64
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:30,260
We originally brought back 842 pounds.

65
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:31,980
And you can see we have cabinets in here.

66
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,920
And these cabinets have nitrogen gas running through them.

67
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,360
The nitrogen protects the rocks

68
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:38,700
from certain elements in Earth's atmosphere.

69
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,100
On the moon, there is no oxygen and there is no water.

70
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,460
The minute the lunar samples were

71
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:46,460
to come in contact with oxygen or water in our atmosphere,

72
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:48,200
they would begin to oxidize.

73
00:03:48,201 --> 00:03:49,700
they would begin to rust.

74
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,440
And the samples in a few decades wouldn't be any good

75
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:55,940
for scientific study.

76
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:58,180
Collecting these geological samples from the moon

77
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:00,580
was a top priority of the Apollo missions.

78
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:07,860
No better clues exist about how the moon formed and evolved.

79
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:10,520
To gather the moon rocks, the astronauts came equipped

80
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:12,120
with custom-designed tools.

81
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,120
These are tongs.

82
00:04:16,121 --> 00:04:18,520
They worked by squeezing the handle,

83
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:20,060
and the tongs would open.

84
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:23,600
And this enabled the astronauts to pick up rocks off the ground

85
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:28,100
because they really couldn't bend over in their spacesuits.

86
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,160
Some of the rocks the astronauts brought back

87
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:32,880
from the moon were similar to those found on Earth.

88
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:37,240
Many were basalt, a product of volcanic activity.

89
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:39,980
There were lavas, and there were crustal rocks,

90
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:43,780
like the kinds of rocks we made granite tombstones out of.

91
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:46,120
As the crewmen gathered the rocks,

92
00:04:46,121 --> 00:04:47,780
on the lunar surface.

93
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:51,420
Other rocks, tiny ones, hurtling through space,

94
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,320
added an element of danger to their mission.

95
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:56,900
Such rocky debris, including meteorites,

96
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,500
also pelts the Earth.

97
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,300
But Earth's atmosphere protects us by disintegrating them

98
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,400
or slowing them down.

99
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:05,500
The moon, which has no atmosphere,

100
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:08,940
exposed the astronauts to the threat.

101
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,720
They come in at such fast speeds, many times the speed

102
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:12,820
of a bullet.

103
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:15,480
And the spacesuits were made in such a way that they could

104
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:17,320
could withstand some of these impacts,

105
00:05:17,321 --> 00:05:21,820
because the mass of these particles is very small.

106
00:05:21,821 --> 00:05:24,280
Despite the danger, none of the Apollo astronauts

107
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:25,620
were injured by the particles.

108
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:30,620
Back on Earth, scientists believed

109
00:05:30,621 --> 00:05:33,140
that the rocks recovered from the moon posed an entirely

110
00:05:33,141 --> 00:05:36,440
different kind of threat.

111
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:38,440
We were concerned that perhaps there were bugs

112
00:05:38,441 --> 00:05:41,680
or some sort of Andromeda strain that might exist on the moon,

113
00:05:41,681 --> 00:05:43,140
but it was a very rare possibility.

114
00:05:43,141 --> 00:05:45,080
We understood that the radiation environment

115
00:05:45,081 --> 00:05:46,920
and the lack of an atmosphere on the moon

116
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:49,980
would make it very difficult for a life to survive.

117
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:52,220
But you always want to be cautious in an unknown

118
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:53,920
environment.

119
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:56,760
Extensive tests determined that the moon rocks contained

120
00:05:56,860 --> 00:06:00,320
no hint of alien life.

121
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:02,840
But as hoped, they have helped researchers

122
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:06,200
gain many new insights.

123
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:10,340
Since basalt is a common rock on both the moon and on Earth,

124
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:14,240
studying its chemistry was the basis for a mind-boggling theory

125
00:06:14,241 --> 00:06:19,980
was formed over 4.5 billion years ago.

126
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:23,220
The leading theory right now for the formation of the moon

127
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:26,560
is that very early in solar system history,

128
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:31,460
a planet or a protoplanet the size of Mars impacted

129
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:33,520
the very early planet Earth.

130
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:36,560
The Mars-sized planet was shattered.

131
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:40,900
The core of that Mars-sized body became part of Earth,

132
00:06:40,901 --> 00:06:45,480
and the exterior parts, the crust and the mantle,

133
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:48,140
were all pulverized, and all those particles

134
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:50,080
went into orbit around the Earth.

135
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:54,020
So for a while, the Earth had a ring system.

136
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:56,680
And then over time, those particles

137
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:59,280
began to slow down and coalesce.

138
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:01,660
And after a while, they had all clumped up,

139
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:04,160
and they became the moon.

140
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:07,100
But what about the six sextillion tons of rock

141
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,560
we call planet Earth?

142
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:13,540
By the way, that's about 1 trillion tons of rock

143
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:16,740
for every person on the planet.

144
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:18,800
What are they?

145
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:21,540
In simple terms, rocks are composed of one or more

146
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:23,140
minerals.

147
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:26,840
Minerals are the most solid material found on Earth.

148
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:30,460
And they always have the same chemical makeup.

149
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:33,760
There are three basic classifications of rock.

150
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:37,200
One is igneous, like the rocks found in the lava fields

151
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,800
of the Hawaiian Islands.

152
00:07:39,801 --> 00:07:43,040
An igneous rock is a rock that's formed from cooled magma,

153
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:46,120
magma being liquid molten rock that has come to the surface

154
00:07:46,121 --> 00:07:48,300
or near the surface like you would see in a volcano.

155
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:51,800
Another type of rock is sedimentary,

156
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:54,180
like that found in the Grand Canyon.

157
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:57,700
Sedimentary rocks are formed by erosion,

158
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,820
making bigger rocks into smaller rocks.

159
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,880
And these smaller rocks, when they lay on top of each other

160
00:08:01,881 --> 00:08:04,260
over many, many years, they cement together

161
00:08:04,261 --> 00:08:06,760
until they form a solid rock, a sedimentary rock.

162
00:08:06,761 --> 00:08:10,760
The third type of rock is metamorphic.

163
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:13,960
Metamorphic rock forms when a preexisting rock type is

164
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:17,500
subjected to heat and extreme pressure.

165
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:22,100
This causes a physical or chemical change in the rock.

166
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:25,420
It could be an igneous rock originally, sedimentary rock

167
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:27,020
originally, or another metamorphic rock.

168
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:29,520
The word metamorphic, meta, means change.

169
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:30,820
Morph means form.

170
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:32,860
So in some fashion, it has changed in form,

171
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:35,020
either through a change in the mineralogy

172
00:08:35,021 --> 00:08:37,760
or the hardness of the rock.

173
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:40,320
It can take billions of years for a rock to morph

174
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:42,620
from one form to another.

175
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:45,660
Yet in our never-ending drive to put rocks to use,

176
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:49,660
we're speeding up the process with technology.

177
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:53,080
Sometimes we can almost do the impossible.

178
00:08:53,180 --> 00:08:57,080
You ever hear the term sink like a rock?

179
00:08:57,180 --> 00:09:00,580
Well, with today's technology, we can reverse that.

180
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,820
We get some products that actually weigh less than water.

181
00:09:03,821 --> 00:09:04,720
in water.

182
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:08,460
So they'll actually float when you put them in water.

183
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:11,720
This is lightweight aggregate, but you won't find it

184
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:14,560
in nature.

185
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:17,840
This rock has been manufactured at the Stalight Company in Gold

186
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:23,900
Hill, North Carolina. Composed of sand, gravel,

187
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,940
and crushed stone, aggregate is a primary ingredient in concrete.

188
00:09:27,941 --> 00:09:32,540
Without it, concrete wouldn't exist,

189
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:35,880
and neither would the modern world as we know it.

190
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:38,580
We couldn't lay foundations strong enough for buildings

191
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:44,820
to scrape the sky, or build titanic dams,

192
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:47,600
or pave the sidewalks leading to our homes.

193
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:52,900
However, all aggregates are not created equal.

194
00:09:52,901 --> 00:09:55,400
equal.

195
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:58,400
Lightweight aggregate composed of such light but strong rocks

196
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:02,240
as meta-argillite can make much lighter weight concrete

197
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:05,440
than traditional aggregate.

198
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:07,540
And lightweight concrete is desirable

199
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:11,060
because it can reduce construction costs.

200
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:13,660
Lightweight aggregate reduces the weight of the concrete

201
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:18,660
by 25% to 30%, which allows you to use a lot less foundation,

202
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:21,900
less reinforcement, less reinforcing steels,

203
00:10:21,901 --> 00:10:26,840
and there's less seismic mass to the foundations as well.

204
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:28,500
The rocks that the Stalight Company

205
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,700
uses to produce the lightweight aggregate

206
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:33,640
come from North Carolina's Gold Hill Quarry,

207
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:37,740
operated by the Vulcan Materials Company.

208
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:39,360
This is our meta-argillite.

209
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:42,760
It is not a slate, actually, but it has a slatey appearance.

210
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:44,360
It's very hard.

211
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:46,700
This rock is so hard it has to be blasted

212
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:49,060
out of the ground.

213
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:51,900
The explosive used is made of ammonium nitrogen.

214
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:55,900
Ammonium nitrate is essentially the same fertilizer that you

215
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,100
put on your yard to make it go.

216
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,880
The explosives are placed in a pattern

217
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:04,540
that will create a domino effect when detonated.

218
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:07,120
These holes are approximately 46 feet deep.

219
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:12,480
We drill on a 15 by 17 foot pattern.

220
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:13,680
There's a booster.

221
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:15,620
Inside the booster will go a cap.

222
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:17,920
The cap is a non-electric cap.

223
00:11:17,921 --> 00:11:22,920
It is set off by a powder substance inside the tube.

224
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:28,760
The amount of rock that's blasted can vary,

225
00:11:28,761 --> 00:11:33,760
but here we usually get about 30,000 tons out of a shot.

226
00:11:33,761 --> 00:11:38,380
First stop for these rocks, the rock crusher.

227
00:11:38,381 --> 00:11:40,380
The rock is dumped onto a feeder.

228
00:11:40,381 --> 00:11:44,880
This feeder is fastened to a set of something called drizzly bars,

229
00:11:44,881 --> 00:11:47,780
and they are like grates with openings between them.

230
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:49,880
These things will feed the rock forward.

231
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:52,220
It allows the smaller rock to fall out

232
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:54,520
and not go through the primary crusher,

233
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:58,120
therefore saving energy.

234
00:11:58,220 --> 00:12:02,540
Up to 7,000 tons of rock are crushed each day.

235
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:05,500
That's the weight of a fleet of almost 4,000

236
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:09,340
midsize automobiles.

237
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:11,400
We have a primary jaw crusher,

238
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:14,880
and it's capable of doing about 1,000 tons an hour.

239
00:12:14,881 --> 00:12:18,480
The operation is much like a jaw or a crankshaft on a motor.

240
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:21,420
After crushing, the rocks tumble through several screens

241
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:27,920
to be sorted and are then sent to a rotary kiln.

242
00:12:28,020 --> 00:12:30,820
The kiln is where modern alchemy turns heavy rock

243
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:34,700
into light, for it is here that the rock material expands

244
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:36,940
under heat without losing strength.

245
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:39,080
As it slowly tumbles through the kiln,

246
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:43,340
the temperature slowly rises up to about 2,100 degrees

247
00:12:43,341 --> 00:12:46,840
and at 2100 degrees, it becomes pyroplastic.

248
00:12:46,841 --> 00:12:49,340
So that means the material is actually starting to soften.

249
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:52,680
And then the gases inside basically sulfur dioxide

250
00:12:52,681 --> 00:12:55,180
and some other gases form, and they try to escape.

251
00:12:55,181 --> 00:12:57,180
And what they do is they create millions

252
00:12:57,181 --> 00:13:00,300
of little non-connected cells, millions of little air bubbles

253
00:13:00,301 --> 00:13:03,300
that are trapped inside the aggregate.

254
00:13:03,301 --> 00:13:05,800
Then the material falls and goes through the cooler,

255
00:13:05,801 --> 00:13:07,100
and it hardens.

256
00:13:07,101 --> 00:13:08,300
That's how it gets its low weight,

257
00:13:08,301 --> 00:13:10,000
because none of the cells are actually connected,

258
00:13:10,001 --> 00:13:13,340
but there's millions of them entrapped inside the aggregate.

259
00:13:13,341 --> 00:13:16,940
Stay Light then sells the cooled aggregate to construction firms

260
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:20,940
all over the world to make lightweight concrete used

261
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:25,580
in skyscrapers, bridges, and other major construction

262
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:26,380
projects.

263
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:32,100
Another kind of rock looms large as a building block

264
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:34,900
of modern civilization.

265
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,840
The slabs that workers carve from massive quarries like this

266
00:13:37,940 --> 00:13:43,340
one will end up in everything from your kitchen to your crypt.

267
00:13:43,341 --> 00:13:50,340
One type of rock dominates our city landscapes, carved from

268
00:13:50,341 --> 00:13:53,340
nature's majesty into finely cut building blocks of countless

269
00:13:53,341 --> 00:13:59,340
classic structures. Granite is synonymous with

270
00:13:59,341 --> 00:14:04,340
hardness and durability. You can count on it to last, from

271
00:14:04,341 --> 00:14:08,340
the facade of the Empire State Building to your glistening

272
00:14:08,341 --> 00:14:13,280
building to a glistening kitchen countertop.

273
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:16,580
And it all comes from quarries like the Rock of Ages

274
00:14:16,680 --> 00:14:19,580
in Barrie, Vermont.

275
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:23,080
Over 500 feet deep, the quarry is one of the largest

276
00:14:23,180 --> 00:14:27,400
in the world, noted for both the quality of its rock

277
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:30,000
and the extent of the deposit.

278
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:33,300
Barry stone itself is just an exceptional granite,

279
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:35,640
probably the finest gray granite yet discovered

280
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:37,700
anywhere in the world.

281
00:14:37,701 --> 00:14:40,340
The deposit's been measured by sound technology.

282
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:43,900
It's approximately 4 miles long, 1 and 1 half to 2 miles wide,

283
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,940
and it's estimated to be up to about 10 miles in thickness.

284
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:51,780
That's a tower of granite the height of over 36

285
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:53,120
Empire State buildings.

286
00:14:55,780 --> 00:14:58,720
The deposit at the Rock of Ages, like all granite,

287
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:00,620
is igneous rock.

288
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:04,120
It formed from magma, generated millions of years ago

289
00:15:04,121 --> 00:15:07,520
by friction between tectonic plates deep below the Earth's

290
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:09,960
surface.

291
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:13,120
Less dense than the solid rock surrounding it,

292
00:15:13,220 --> 00:15:15,200
the molten material rose up through cracks

293
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,300
in the overlying rock and cooled into the huge granite

294
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:19,140
deposit.

295
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:25,340
Granite from the Rock of Ages Quarry

296
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:27,880
has been used in many of America's greatest buildings

297
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:29,780
and monuments.

298
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:33,580
We were very, very proud to be a part of the fabrication

299
00:15:33,581 --> 00:15:36,080
of the National World War II Memorial that is now

300
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:38,820
on the Mall in Washington, D.C.

301
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:42,420
The steps of the Capitol building in Washington, D.C.

302
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:46,620
are also fabricated from berry gray granite.

303
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:48,420
Rocks such as granite and marble

304
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:52,940
are often used as so-called dimension stones.

305
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:55,240
The term dimension stone refers to stone

306
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:58,380
that's cut to be a certain dimensional size

307
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:01,680
rather than aggregate that's to be used for crushed stone

308
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:03,580
and other purposes.

309
00:16:03,581 --> 00:16:07,180
Often it's taken out of the ground in giant blocks,

310
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:10,820
weighing as much as 200 tons.

311
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:14,820
Because granite is so hard, it takes giant, powerful drills

312
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:16,520
and saws to cut into it.

313
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:21,160
Quarrymen call the process of separating the granite

314
00:16:21,260 --> 00:16:24,000
into blocks channeling.

315
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:26,340
To separate a block from the quarry wall,

316
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:29,900
they first have to cut around the sides and the back of the block.

317
00:16:29,901 --> 00:16:32,740
block.

318
00:16:32,840 --> 00:16:35,740
One method uses a slot drill.

319
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:38,840
A slot drill is an air-driven rotary drill.

320
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:42,520
It is set up so that it drills a vertical hole up to about 20

321
00:16:42,620 --> 00:16:43,440
feet in depth.

322
00:16:43,540 --> 00:16:45,720
Then the drill rod retracts automatically, moves over

323
00:16:45,820 --> 00:16:46,820
on a tracking mechanism.

324
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:50,420
We sink another hole in another until we have a row of closely

325
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:53,460
spaced holes up one side, across the back,

326
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:56,220
and down the other side.

327
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:58,960
Once workers drill these initial sets of holes,

328
00:16:58,961 --> 00:17:01,260
They make another pass at the rock,

329
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:04,460
drilling out the granite between the holes, called the web.

330
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:09,540
But now comes the hard part.

331
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:11,000
They've got to separate the bottom

332
00:17:11,100 --> 00:17:14,180
without destroying their equipment.

333
00:17:14,280 --> 00:17:17,180
The process is called undercutting.

334
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:19,140
It begins by drilling a series of holes

335
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:21,320
in the bottom of the block and will

336
00:17:21,420 --> 00:17:24,280
end with a huge explosion.

337
00:17:24,380 --> 00:17:27,560
We use primer cord that's often used as a fuse

338
00:17:27,561 --> 00:17:28,560
in other industries.

339
00:17:28,660 --> 00:17:30,200
It looks like a giant jump rope.

340
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:32,200
It's on a large reel like a wire.

341
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:35,000
It's reamed into the holes with a metal rod,

342
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:36,160
about every other hole.

343
00:17:36,260 --> 00:17:39,440
Then it's tied together electrically and set off

344
00:17:39,540 --> 00:17:41,300
remotely.

345
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:42,100
Fire!

346
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:48,840
Once loosened, the slabs are lifted to the rim of the quarry

347
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:52,820
by giant derricks, or cranes.

348
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:54,160
The most powerful of the derricks

349
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:57,560
can lift an astounding 200 tons of stone at a time.

350
00:17:57,561 --> 00:17:59,560
out of the 500-foot-deep quarry.

351
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:05,100
We lower cable from a derrick and put it in a loop

352
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,300
around the perimeter of the stone.

353
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:08,300
We don't go underneath the stone, because we would have

354
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:11,200
no way of lifting it up to put the cable underneath it.

355
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:13,100
So we go around the perimeter, actually

356
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:15,920
cut a small notch into each of the four corners of the stone,

357
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:18,420
and draw the cable tightly, just like a slipknot,

358
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:21,320
so that the harder that the block pulls on the cable,

359
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:23,320
the tighter it becomes.

360
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:25,920
Next, the granite goes to a processing plant

361
00:18:25,921 --> 00:18:29,520
to be cut and polished.

362
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:32,320
This is where workers craft it into the dimension stones

363
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:33,920
used for our buildings and homes.

364
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:42,400
The most unusual place the granite might show up

365
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:46,180
is six feet over your head.

366
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:49,440
Craftsmen first work out tombstone design and lettering

367
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:50,740
on a computer.

368
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:54,640
Then that design is transferred from paper

369
00:18:54,641 --> 00:18:59,640
to a rubber sheet by the computer.

370
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:02,640
The rubber sheet is then temporarily adhered or glued

371
00:19:02,740 --> 00:19:05,380
to the surface of the granite, and parts of it

372
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:09,280
are cut away to form a stencil.

373
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:12,800
A sandblaster then takes over, spewing its abrasive

374
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:14,600
under high pressure.

375
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:18,760
At 125 pounds per square inch, even granite gives way

376
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:20,900
under this assault.

377
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,900
The abrasive actually will hit the rubber,

378
00:19:23,901 --> 00:19:26,440
but because it can absorb some of the energy,

379
00:19:26,540 --> 00:19:30,640
it deflects and it bounces away.

380
00:19:30,740 --> 00:19:35,140
Another stone that stands the test of time is marble.

381
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:36,940
Whether used in great works of art

382
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:39,340
like Michelangelo's Statue of David,

383
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:42,360
or classic buildings like the United States Capitol,

384
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:46,060
or the Lincoln Memorial, it's been a favorite of artists

385
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:47,460
and architects for centuries.

386
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:52,660
Marble is such a desirable stone

387
00:19:52,661 --> 00:19:57,160
because it unifies two very important things,

388
00:19:57,260 --> 00:20:00,660
the beauty and the strength.

389
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:03,900
Most marble quarries are above ground.

390
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,500
But the Vermont Marble Company's mine in Danby, Vermont,

391
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:10,020
is the largest underground marble quarry in the world.

392
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:16,820
The marble supply here reaches over one mile into the earth

393
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:18,820
and is spread over 25 acres.

394
00:20:18,821 --> 00:20:21,820
acres.

395
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:24,360
Marble is a metamorphic rock formed

396
00:20:24,460 --> 00:20:27,060
by the alteration of limestone or dolomite.

397
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:34,840
It's so hard, they use diamond wire saws to cut it.

398
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:37,200
Diamonds are the hardest of all rocks,

399
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:41,540
and one of the few strong enough to cut through marble.

400
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:44,980
The diamonds are strong on a flexible wire.

401
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:46,620
We put it on a certain sequence,

402
00:20:46,621 --> 00:20:50,920
And we start with a spring, and we slide it on the cable.

403
00:20:51,020 --> 00:20:52,660
And then we use a spacer.

404
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:56,260
And then we use a pearl, what we call a pearl because it's

405
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:59,760
round and it's expensive, and it's got diamonds.

406
00:20:59,860 --> 00:21:05,780
And we do another spacer, spring, spacer, a pearl.

407
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:11,720
When the diamond saws blur into motion,

408
00:21:11,820 --> 00:21:14,520
the workers keep their distance in case the wire breaks.

409
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:17,860
It's very dangerous work.

410
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:19,580
We have to be careful where you're standing,

411
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:22,920
because when the wire breaks, you could get hit with pieces

412
00:21:22,921 --> 00:21:25,460
coming off the wire, moving at a high rate of speed.

413
00:21:26,760 --> 00:21:29,540
The workers select only the highest grade of stone.

414
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:34,140
What you're looking at here is the face of the gallery side area,

415
00:21:34,780 --> 00:21:38,100
and that black and gray and brown streak you see in there

416
00:21:38,101 --> 00:21:39,400
is what we call tunnel rock.

417
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:41,840
It's not the desirable stuff that we're after.

418
00:21:42,480 --> 00:21:44,360
This is actually the stuff that we desire.

419
00:21:44,361 --> 00:21:47,100
This particular block right here is what we call an imperial

420
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:48,100
marble.

421
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:52,160
It's some of the finest marble in the world.

422
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:55,400
Pure white marble is the result of the metamorphism

423
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:59,100
of very pure limestone.

424
00:21:59,180 --> 00:22:01,840
When mineral impurities are present in the limestone,

425
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:04,620
they can produce the characteristic swirls and veins

426
00:22:04,680 --> 00:22:06,720
in many varieties of colored marble.

427
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:14,360
Blocks sliced from the wall can weigh as much as 1,000 pounds

428
00:22:14,361 --> 00:22:20,360
and are worth about $10,000 before being processed.

429
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:23,600
Workers cut them down to about 45,000 pounds

430
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:25,800
to make them more manageable during transport

431
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:27,100
to the processing plant.

432
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:32,520
It, too, is underground.

433
00:22:32,620 --> 00:22:35,420
Here, the marble is cut to the exact dimensions specified

434
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:37,120
by customers.

435
00:22:37,220 --> 00:22:39,960
Then it is sent to the polisher.

436
00:22:40,060 --> 00:22:42,280
It has 14 different heads on it,

437
00:22:42,281 --> 00:22:45,280
heads on it, and it has different abrasives

438
00:22:45,380 --> 00:22:47,860
that are pulled onto the head, and then the marble is fed

439
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:50,220
through on a conveyor.

440
00:22:50,320 --> 00:22:53,560
And the heads come down, and each one does its part.

441
00:22:53,660 --> 00:22:55,100
And when it comes out the other side,

442
00:22:55,200 --> 00:23:01,500
you can either have what you call a hone or a glassy finish.

443
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:03,440
In the past, the quarry stone has

444
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:06,800
been ordered for both the Jefferson Memorial

445
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:08,840
and the United States Supreme Court.

446
00:23:08,841 --> 00:23:14,840
Yet another type of rock holds the precious stuff industry

447
00:23:14,940 --> 00:23:17,480
uses to make everything from your car,

448
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:21,080
to your appliances, to your paper clips.

449
00:23:21,180 --> 00:23:24,080
But prying it loose requires a lot of noise.

450
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:25,080
Fire!

451
00:23:27,900 --> 00:23:30,500
Water.

452
00:23:30,600 --> 00:23:31,700
And heat.

453
00:23:31,701 --> 00:23:41,040
Our modern world is built on a foundation of iron.

454
00:23:41,140 --> 00:23:43,040
Iron is used to make steel.

455
00:23:43,140 --> 00:23:45,880
We would not have all of the factories, the appliances,

456
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:47,080
the cars.

457
00:23:47,180 --> 00:23:49,680
None of the things that we know today in modern civilization

458
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:53,360
would exist basically without iron and iron ore.

459
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:57,300
And iron comes from rock, like this.

460
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:00,000
When a rock is valuable enough to be mined for the metals

461
00:24:00,001 --> 00:24:02,000
for the metals or minerals trapped within.

462
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:03,000
It's called ore.

463
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:09,000
Minnesota is one of the most iron-rich states.

464
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:11,740
Minnesota was blessed with a large deposit of iron

465
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:13,840
called the Biwabic Iron Formation.

466
00:24:13,940 --> 00:24:17,220
And it extends for about 110 miles long from Babbitt,

467
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:20,460
Minnesota down to Grand Rapids, Minnesota.

468
00:24:20,560 --> 00:24:24,160
The iron ore began forming over 2 billion years ago,

469
00:24:24,260 --> 00:24:25,860
when the area that's now Minnesota

470
00:24:25,960 --> 00:24:29,160
was covered by a shallow sea.

471
00:24:29,161 --> 00:24:33,160
The iron source was located to the north of the iron range

472
00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:35,460
and was from volcanic material.

473
00:24:35,560 --> 00:24:40,400
And that was deposited into a water-filled basin

474
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:43,400
and later buried and heated and formed

475
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:47,820
into a hard iron formation rock.

476
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:51,620
Since the late 19th century, 4 billion tons of ore

477
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:54,160
have been mined from the Biowabic formation.

478
00:24:54,260 --> 00:24:59,140
Iron mining began in 1892 near the town of Mount Iron

479
00:24:59,160 --> 00:25:02,760
Then from that point on, more and more mining came into place.

480
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:04,260
The initial mines were underground.

481
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:07,400
They later turned into open pit mines.

482
00:25:07,500 --> 00:25:09,900
The most valuable iron ore during the early days

483
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:16,620
of Minnesota mining was hematite, which is nearly 60% iron.

484
00:25:16,720 --> 00:25:21,220
Within six decades, miners had exhausted the rich supply.

485
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:24,260
It went through World War I, World War II.

486
00:25:24,360 --> 00:25:27,920
Vast quantities of iron ore were mined to provide steel

487
00:25:27,921 --> 00:25:31,760
for the battleships and the tanks and everything else

488
00:25:31,860 --> 00:25:33,660
that went along with those two war efforts.

489
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:36,660
And in the process, of course, a lot of the natural iron ore,

490
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:38,460
the stuff that you could just mine out of the ground,

491
00:25:38,560 --> 00:25:40,360
was exhausted.

492
00:25:40,460 --> 00:25:44,440
So in the early 1950s, a new process was developed

493
00:25:44,540 --> 00:25:47,280
called the taconite process.

494
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:50,000
With about 22% iron content,

495
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:53,040
taconite ore is nowhere near as rich as hematite,

496
00:25:53,140 --> 00:25:55,580
which could be loaded directly from the ground

497
00:25:55,581 --> 00:25:57,980
into steel-making blast furnaces.

498
00:26:01,980 --> 00:26:04,980
With taconite, the iron content must be extracted grain

499
00:26:05,080 --> 00:26:08,320
by grain and then concentrated.

500
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:12,340
Key to the process is taconite's magnetic qualities.

501
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:14,940
An important characteristic of the taconite that's being mined

502
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:18,040
is that the iron is magnetic, and it can be separated from

503
00:26:18,140 --> 00:26:21,680
the non-magnetic material very easily through magnetic separation.

504
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:24,480
To illustrate that point, I'll hold a magnet onto the black

505
00:26:24,481 --> 00:26:27,320
material here, and you will see that it sticks.

506
00:26:27,420 --> 00:26:29,880
The white material, which is quartz, is non-magnetic,

507
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:32,480
and the magnet falls off.

508
00:26:32,580 --> 00:26:34,420
The process of magnetic separation

509
00:26:34,520 --> 00:26:38,520
begins by blasting the stone from out of the ground.

510
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:40,960
The procedure is similar to that used for blasting

511
00:26:41,060 --> 00:26:42,640
aggregate loose.

512
00:26:42,740 --> 00:26:47,240
4, 3, 2, 1, 0, fire.

513
00:26:54,480 --> 00:26:58,920
Large haul trucks carry the blasted rock to a processing plant.

514
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:01,720
This is where we take the blasted ore.

515
00:27:01,820 --> 00:27:03,520
We dump it into this crusher.

516
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:07,020
It's a giant gyrating cone that slowly turns.

517
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:11,200
And as it turns, it crushes the ore against the side wall,

518
00:27:11,300 --> 00:27:14,140
reduces it in size from this blasted material

519
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:17,640
down to about minus 6 inches.

520
00:27:17,740 --> 00:27:20,440
From here, the process involves reducing the rocks

521
00:27:20,540 --> 00:27:23,680
to smaller and smaller bits, so the magnetic iron can be

522
00:27:23,681 --> 00:27:27,180
can be extracted.

523
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:30,120
From the crusher, the rocks go through a series of mills

524
00:27:30,220 --> 00:27:31,820
inside the processing plant.

525
00:27:31,920 --> 00:27:33,920
So the first stage of grinding is called the rod mill.

526
00:27:34,020 --> 00:27:36,520
And what we do there is we introduce the crushed ore

527
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:39,220
with water and put it into a slurry form, which

528
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:42,340
is just a mixture of water with the ground material.

529
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:44,840
All of our processing is done wet,

530
00:27:44,940 --> 00:27:47,240
so we have to mix water with whatever material we have

531
00:27:47,340 --> 00:27:49,340
to transport it through the process.

532
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:52,480
And then we feed it into these large mills that rotate and tumble.

533
00:27:52,481 --> 00:27:55,620
Inside of these mills, we put large diameter grinding rods.

534
00:27:55,720 --> 00:27:58,580
These are about 4 inches in diameter and 20 feet long.

535
00:27:58,680 --> 00:28:01,560
And as these rods tumble over with the turning of the mill,

536
00:28:01,660 --> 00:28:05,300
they grind the ore into a finer slurry.

537
00:28:05,400 --> 00:28:07,360
Then the slurry goes through its first set

538
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:10,340
of magnetic separators, which attract particles

539
00:28:10,440 --> 00:28:12,840
with at least 100 times more power

540
00:28:12,940 --> 00:28:15,600
than the magnet on your refrigerator at home.

541
00:28:15,700 --> 00:28:18,300
Magnetic separators are large rotating drums that have

542
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:20,140
permanent magnets inside of them.

543
00:28:20,141 --> 00:28:22,600
The magnetic portion of the ground material

544
00:28:22,700 --> 00:28:28,280
is then picked up and separated from the non-magnetic portion.

545
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,880
The particles are then sent to ball mills

546
00:28:30,980 --> 00:28:34,480
that grind them down to the consistency of face powder.

547
00:28:34,580 --> 00:28:37,300
We use 1 1⁄2 inch diameter grinding balls, which

548
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:38,400
are fed into the mill.

549
00:28:38,500 --> 00:28:42,600
And as they tumble, they grind the ore even finer.

550
00:28:42,700 --> 00:28:44,140
Not ready yet.

551
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:45,800
The material then goes through another set

552
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:47,900
of magnetic separators.

553
00:28:47,901 --> 00:28:52,540
It emerges as a concentrate of about 67% iron.

554
00:28:52,640 --> 00:28:54,740
At that point in time, we then start

555
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:57,940
adding some limestone and dolomite back into the process

556
00:28:58,040 --> 00:29:01,740
to make a very special pellet for our customer.

557
00:29:01,840 --> 00:29:04,280
These pellets are the form in which the iron will be fed

558
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:07,860
into the blast furnaces.

559
00:29:07,960 --> 00:29:10,260
But first, the concentrate must be dried.

560
00:29:10,360 --> 00:29:13,420
In that plant, we used vacuum disk dryers

561
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:17,900
to actually suck the moisture out of this wet concentrate.

562
00:29:17,901 --> 00:29:20,800
An air blast loosens the particles.

563
00:29:20,900 --> 00:29:24,080
That concentrate is then fed into what we call balling

564
00:29:24,180 --> 00:29:28,080
disks, and we spin these disks and we create these pellets,

565
00:29:28,180 --> 00:29:30,640
which we call green balls.

566
00:29:30,740 --> 00:29:33,340
Then the pellets are baked in a giant furnace.

567
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:36,880
And this is a long 260-foot furnace

568
00:29:36,980 --> 00:29:40,860
where the pellets are fired at 2,400 degrees Fahrenheit

569
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:43,660
and then cooled down as they exit the process.

570
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:47,300
The pellets have to be hardened to a certain strength in order

571
00:29:47,400 --> 00:29:48,100
for them to be able to get the ball

572
00:29:48,101 --> 00:29:51,900
to withstand the transportation that occurs between here

573
00:29:51,901 --> 00:29:54,700
and the blast furnace.

574
00:29:54,701 --> 00:29:57,340
The pellets are now over 60% iron.

575
00:29:57,341 --> 00:29:59,220
When the pellets come off the end of the furnace,

576
00:29:59,221 --> 00:30:00,280
they're quite hot yet.

577
00:30:00,281 --> 00:30:02,520
And when they enter into the stockpile behind us,

578
00:30:02,521 --> 00:30:05,620
they're still at a couple of hundred degrees.

579
00:30:05,621 --> 00:30:07,860
From here, the pellets are loaded into rail cars

580
00:30:07,861 --> 00:30:10,280
and shipped down to a port on Lake Superior.

581
00:30:10,281 --> 00:30:12,100
From there, the pellets are loaded into boats,

582
00:30:12,101 --> 00:30:14,360
where they begin their journey down to the blast furnaces

583
00:30:14,361 --> 00:30:15,700
at the southern end of Lake Michigan.

584
00:30:17,900 --> 00:30:21,700
In the blast furnaces, the pellets are melted into molten

585
00:30:21,800 --> 00:30:22,500
iron.

586
00:30:25,500 --> 00:30:29,020
Some furnaces using the pellets can produce over 10,000 tons

587
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:30,640
of molten iron a day.

588
00:30:32,680 --> 00:30:36,020
From here, the molten iron will go to foundries,

589
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:39,660
where the steel is made to build our world.

590
00:30:39,760 --> 00:30:42,600
As rocks rich with iron demand a complex process

591
00:30:42,700 --> 00:30:46,720
to extract their treasure, another kind of iron

592
00:30:46,721 --> 00:30:50,660
Another kind of invaluable rock comes prefabricated by nature.

593
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:54,960
Grab your toys.

594
00:30:55,060 --> 00:30:56,860
It's time to play in the sandbox.

595
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:05,700
When it comes to rocks, bigger doesn't always mean better.

596
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:08,700
The smallest rocks of all, sand and gravel,

597
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:11,040
are crucial ingredients in construction projects

598
00:31:11,140 --> 00:31:15,220
requiring asphalt or concrete.

599
00:31:15,221 --> 00:31:20,060
Typically, asphalt is in the range of 95% stone products.

600
00:31:20,160 --> 00:31:22,560
Concrete is about 80%.

601
00:31:22,660 --> 00:31:26,960
For a single-family home, it's about 400 tons of stone.

602
00:31:27,060 --> 00:31:29,560
It's estimated that 38,000 tons of aggregate

603
00:31:29,660 --> 00:31:32,840
are necessary to construct one mile of a four-lane interstate

604
00:31:32,940 --> 00:31:33,740
highway.

605
00:31:37,640 --> 00:31:41,520
Crushed rock, sand, and gravel, and lightweight aggregate

606
00:31:41,620 --> 00:31:45,220
have been essential building materials since ancient times.

607
00:31:45,221 --> 00:31:49,100
From a historical perspective, you look back, basically all

608
00:31:49,200 --> 00:31:51,960
the construction has been based on using crushed stone, sand,

609
00:31:52,060 --> 00:31:54,800
and gravel type products from the early Roman roads

610
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:56,200
to today's interstates.

611
00:31:56,300 --> 00:31:59,460
Basically, our nation and our economy

612
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:04,500
are based on a solid foundation of construction aggregates.

613
00:32:04,600 --> 00:32:07,380
Unlike rock borings, where we rely on explosives

614
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:12,120
to blast the aggregate loose, the deposits in many sand and

615
00:32:12,220 --> 00:32:15,220
gravel areas are not used to be used to be used to be used to

616
00:32:15,221 --> 00:32:18,420
come ready-made by Mother Nature.

617
00:32:18,520 --> 00:32:20,720
They're situated where the loose rock has existed

618
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:23,920
since prehistoric times, like here

619
00:32:24,020 --> 00:32:26,260
at Vulcan Materials Puddle Dock Quarry

620
00:32:26,360 --> 00:32:28,160
in Prince George County, Virginia.

621
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:34,680
About 100 million years ago, a river ran through this area,

622
00:32:34,780 --> 00:32:37,780
leaving layers of rock along its shores.

623
00:32:37,880 --> 00:32:40,880
Weathering breaks the rock down into various size fractions.

624
00:32:40,980 --> 00:32:43,380
As they are moved along the river channels,

625
00:32:43,381 --> 00:32:48,720
they are rounded and broken into smaller and smaller sizes.

626
00:32:48,820 --> 00:32:50,080
With each passing century,

627
00:32:50,180 --> 00:32:53,220
the river deposited more and more layers of loose sand

628
00:32:53,320 --> 00:32:54,120
and gravel.

629
00:32:56,320 --> 00:32:57,920
The excavator is loading material

630
00:32:58,020 --> 00:32:59,320
that's not been blasted.

631
00:32:59,420 --> 00:33:03,540
It's a loose material that we can dig quite easily.

632
00:33:03,640 --> 00:33:06,980
Sand is composed of rocks such as feldspar, limestone,

633
00:33:07,080 --> 00:33:09,340
and quartz.

634
00:33:09,440 --> 00:33:12,280
Gravel consists of pebbles, stones, and fragments

635
00:33:12,281 --> 00:33:17,280
and fragments of such minerals as shale and granite.

636
00:33:17,380 --> 00:33:21,920
We mine the sand and gravel with a 5.6-yard cubic excavator.

637
00:33:22,020 --> 00:33:24,520
Our haul trucks are 40-ton articulated trucks.

638
00:33:24,620 --> 00:33:27,460
On a good day, we can average between 8,000 and 10,000 tons

639
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:29,640
with this operation.

640
00:33:29,740 --> 00:33:32,000
We haul the material to the surge pile,

641
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:34,300
dozer pushes it over, and a loader picks it up

642
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:37,340
and puts it into the feed hopper.

643
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:39,540
The feed hopper distributes the sand and gravel

644
00:33:39,640 --> 00:33:41,380
onto a huge conveyor belt.

645
00:33:41,381 --> 00:33:45,880
that transports it to the main processing plant.

646
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:47,680
With the price of diesel fuel going up,

647
00:33:47,780 --> 00:33:49,880
we didn't want to have to haul the material over a mile.

648
00:33:49,980 --> 00:33:52,920
So we installed almost a mile of conveyor belt that will

649
00:33:53,020 --> 00:33:56,920
carry approximately 1,000 tons an hour.

650
00:33:57,020 --> 00:33:59,140
At the processing plant, a vibrating machine

651
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:01,340
with a series of sifting screens

652
00:34:01,440 --> 00:34:04,340
separates the sand from the gravel.

653
00:34:04,440 --> 00:34:06,440
Once the material hits the number one screen,

654
00:34:06,540 --> 00:34:09,540
that material is then sized according to whether it goes

655
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:12,540
into the gravel or the sand circuit.

656
00:34:12,640 --> 00:34:14,540
Each of the screens has a smaller mesh

657
00:34:14,640 --> 00:34:17,040
than the one above it.

658
00:34:17,140 --> 00:34:19,540
The larger gravel rocks stay at the top,

659
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:23,280
and the smaller sand particles drop to the bottom.

660
00:34:23,380 --> 00:34:26,300
That begins the process of sorting the material by size.

661
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:27,800
It's essentially like this.

662
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:38,800
The material goes across the first screen

663
00:34:38,801 --> 00:34:41,260
and then goes through a series of additional screens

664
00:34:41,360 --> 00:34:45,040
and is sorted by size in decreasing diameter.

665
00:34:49,540 --> 00:34:51,540
Once separated from the gravel,

666
00:34:51,640 --> 00:34:54,140
the sand is sent through an additional screening process

667
00:34:54,240 --> 00:34:56,160
in water-filled classifying tanks.

668
00:34:58,560 --> 00:35:01,560
Much like panning for gold, the finer sand particles

669
00:35:01,660 --> 00:35:03,960
rise to the top of the water separator,

670
00:35:04,060 --> 00:35:07,060
and the heavier ones drop to the bottom.

671
00:35:07,061 --> 00:35:10,500
This is the fatter sand that we pull out of our core sand.

672
00:35:10,600 --> 00:35:14,200
We then take this material, let it go by gravity back down

673
00:35:14,300 --> 00:35:16,300
to the ground level, pump it back up again,

674
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:18,800
and resize it even further.

675
00:35:18,900 --> 00:35:20,900
After processing, the sand and gravel

676
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:23,720
are ready to be shipped.

677
00:35:23,820 --> 00:35:26,620
The construction aggregate business is so competitive

678
00:35:26,720 --> 00:35:29,620
that shipping costs are a major concern.

679
00:35:29,720 --> 00:35:32,420
Therefore, most quarries are located close to construction

680
00:35:32,520 --> 00:35:34,920
site areas.

681
00:35:34,921 --> 00:35:38,260
Construction aggers are typically used within 20 to 30 miles

682
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:40,460
of their point of production.

683
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:43,920
That is, unless there are no local suppliers.

684
00:35:44,020 --> 00:35:48,460
Then the material will have to be shipped longer distances.

685
00:35:48,560 --> 00:35:52,480
In this case, a barge is the likely transport.

686
00:35:52,580 --> 00:35:55,180
This pit is adjacent to the Appomattox River.

687
00:35:55,280 --> 00:35:57,280
We ship quite a bit of our material on barges

688
00:35:57,380 --> 00:36:00,420
down to the Norfolk area.

689
00:36:00,520 --> 00:36:03,280
Finished product is loaded on our barge loadout facility

690
00:36:03,281 --> 00:36:06,880
here behind me, dump trucks dumping in a grizzly hopper

691
00:36:06,980 --> 00:36:09,180
up the conveyor belt onto the barge.

692
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:14,020
This barge will hold approximately 2,000 tons.

693
00:36:14,120 --> 00:36:18,620
The aggregate is often shipped to concrete plants

694
00:36:18,720 --> 00:36:22,260
and then set off to make our churches, swimming pools,

695
00:36:22,360 --> 00:36:23,340
and shopping malls.

696
00:36:25,900 --> 00:36:30,000
Although rocks are most useful as building materials,

697
00:36:30,001 --> 00:36:34,480
They may soon rock our world in a surprising new way.

698
00:36:34,580 --> 00:36:36,240
We're starting to light up our cities

699
00:36:36,340 --> 00:36:40,280
with the power of hot rocks not so deep beneath our feet.

700
00:36:45,220 --> 00:36:48,180
The world is looking for sources of clean, reliable,

701
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:50,560
and renewable energy.

702
00:36:50,660 --> 00:36:53,820
Northern California has found it.

703
00:36:53,920 --> 00:36:56,320
We're in the Mayocamas Mountains of California's

704
00:36:56,321 --> 00:37:00,520
of California's coast range at the Geyser's power plants.

705
00:37:00,521 --> 00:37:03,620
The Geyser's power plants are geothermal power plants

706
00:37:03,621 --> 00:37:05,960
that cover 40 square miles of the mountains here

707
00:37:05,961 --> 00:37:10,260
and generate enough electricity to provide 850,000 households

708
00:37:10,261 --> 00:37:12,260
with electric power.

709
00:37:12,261 --> 00:37:15,680
It's the largest geothermal area's force producing power

710
00:37:15,681 --> 00:37:17,880
in the world.

711
00:37:17,881 --> 00:37:21,020
And where does this geothermal energy come from?

712
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:23,080
Hot rocks.

713
00:37:23,081 --> 00:37:26,080
In most places, molten rock, or magma,

714
00:37:26,180 --> 00:37:28,520
exists very deep in the Earth, where temperatures

715
00:37:28,620 --> 00:37:31,180
are extremely high.

716
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:34,320
The geyser's area is unique in that the magma is very close

717
00:37:34,420 --> 00:37:36,020
to the Earth's surface.

718
00:37:36,120 --> 00:37:37,820
The heat that supplies the geyser

719
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:42,140
is supplied from liquid magma about five miles deep.

720
00:37:42,240 --> 00:37:45,340
This liquid magma is left over from a volcanic period that

721
00:37:45,440 --> 00:37:47,880
existed here about 1.3 million years ago.

722
00:37:47,980 --> 00:37:49,480
That volcanism is long since gone,

723
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:53,080
but it's left behind these pools of magma.

724
00:37:53,081 --> 00:37:55,780
In some areas of the geysers, this heat bubbles right up

725
00:37:55,880 --> 00:37:57,320
to the surface.

726
00:37:57,420 --> 00:37:59,720
This is a steam vent, also known as a fumarole.

727
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:02,120
And it's evidence that we're very close to a geothermal

728
00:38:02,220 --> 00:38:03,120
resource here.

729
00:38:03,220 --> 00:38:05,520
Steam exits that vent at 250 degrees,

730
00:38:05,620 --> 00:38:07,120
causing that water to boil.

731
00:38:07,220 --> 00:38:11,440
If you fell in there, you could get boiled alive.

732
00:38:11,540 --> 00:38:13,600
The owners of the Calpine Corporation

733
00:38:13,700 --> 00:38:16,800
operate most of the geysers' power plants.

734
00:38:16,900 --> 00:38:18,640
But they are the first ones to take advantage

735
00:38:18,740 --> 00:38:20,140
of this unique place.

736
00:38:20,240 --> 00:38:22,280
This area was known to the Indians thousands of years

737
00:38:22,281 --> 00:38:23,480
when they lived here.

738
00:38:23,481 --> 00:38:25,480
They utilized the hot springs for hot steam

739
00:38:25,481 --> 00:38:27,280
and for hot water.

740
00:38:27,281 --> 00:38:30,480
Later on, in about 1847, an explorer named William Bell

741
00:38:30,481 --> 00:38:33,620
Elliott happened on this area and was really quite surprised

742
00:38:33,621 --> 00:38:35,320
to see steam venting out of the ground

743
00:38:35,321 --> 00:38:37,320
and hot, bubbling mud coming up.

744
00:38:37,321 --> 00:38:38,960
And so he returned to his companions,

745
00:38:38,961 --> 00:38:42,660
exclaiming that he'd found the gates of hell.

746
00:38:42,661 --> 00:38:45,560
During the 1920s, several attempts were made

747
00:38:45,561 --> 00:38:47,980
to tap the geothermal energy resources here

748
00:38:47,981 --> 00:38:50,340
for electrical power.

749
00:38:50,341 --> 00:38:53,580
But it wasn't until the 1950s that drilling technology

750
00:38:53,680 --> 00:38:58,380
became advanced enough to make the resource truly productive.

751
00:38:58,480 --> 00:39:00,980
They started drilling thermal wells deep into the Earth's

752
00:39:01,080 --> 00:39:04,580
core to capture the steam and utilize it to generate power.

753
00:39:04,680 --> 00:39:07,700
From there, in the 60s, the first plant was built.

754
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:11,360
And since then, they're built up to 23 plants that have operated

755
00:39:11,460 --> 00:39:14,700
up here in the geysers almost for 50 years.

756
00:39:14,800 --> 00:39:16,340
The wells at the geysers don't have

757
00:39:16,440 --> 00:39:19,200
to reach all the way down to the liquid magma,

758
00:39:19,201 --> 00:39:21,360
but only to where the rocks are hot enough,

759
00:39:21,460 --> 00:39:24,140
and there is enough water to create a large supply of steam.

760
00:39:26,940 --> 00:39:30,240
Nevertheless, many of the wells are drilled over two miles

761
00:39:30,340 --> 00:39:32,940
into the ground until they reach sandstone.

762
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:35,140
The sandstone's been heated, and it has water in it

763
00:39:35,240 --> 00:39:36,360
that turned into steam.

764
00:39:36,460 --> 00:39:39,060
But on top of that sandstone is what's called a cap rock.

765
00:39:39,160 --> 00:39:41,860
And that cap rock holds all that heat and steam pressure

766
00:39:41,960 --> 00:39:43,060
down in the rock.

767
00:39:43,160 --> 00:39:44,700
We drill down through that cap rock

768
00:39:44,800 --> 00:39:46,860
and into what we call a geotomo reservoir.

769
00:39:46,960 --> 00:39:48,960
And that reservoir is highly fractured,

770
00:39:48,961 --> 00:39:52,200
that allow that steam to travel essentially through the rock

771
00:39:52,300 --> 00:39:54,700
and then into our well-bore pipe.

772
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:57,200
The drilling equipment is identical to that used

773
00:39:57,300 --> 00:40:00,200
for oil and gas wells.

774
00:40:00,300 --> 00:40:03,040
It's kind of drilling that we're in right now.

775
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:05,440
We're in hard rock drilling very deep.

776
00:40:05,540 --> 00:40:07,640
We use these tungsten carbide bits here.

777
00:40:07,740 --> 00:40:09,080
These are the cutting edges here.

778
00:40:09,180 --> 00:40:10,620
It's very, very hard.

779
00:40:10,720 --> 00:40:13,320
It was very, very long time.

780
00:40:13,420 --> 00:40:15,520
Once drilled, the steam is channeled

781
00:40:15,620 --> 00:40:17,580
into an intricate network of pipelines,

782
00:40:17,581 --> 00:40:20,580
stretching over 100 miles.

783
00:40:20,680 --> 00:40:23,580
This is a geothermal well head connected to a steam well

784
00:40:23,680 --> 00:40:25,580
that extends two miles underground.

785
00:40:25,680 --> 00:40:29,320
The steam exits this well head at 350 degrees Fahrenheit,

786
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:32,220
and the steam is transported down the pipeline at 70 miles

787
00:40:32,320 --> 00:40:34,200
an hour to the power plant.

788
00:40:34,300 --> 00:40:37,160
The pipe itself originates from rock material,

789
00:40:37,260 --> 00:40:39,640
primarily iron ore.

790
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:41,200
The pipe's made out of iron.

791
00:40:41,300 --> 00:40:43,340
When iron gets hot, it expands.

792
00:40:43,440 --> 00:40:45,880
When the iron starts out cold when it's first installed,

793
00:40:45,881 --> 00:40:49,220
it might only be 35, 40 degrees here at the geysers,

794
00:40:49,320 --> 00:40:51,520
and this is 350 degrees right here,

795
00:40:51,620 --> 00:40:54,460
so the pipe has to be allowed to expand and contract.

796
00:40:54,560 --> 00:40:56,020
And that's why everything is mounted

797
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,000
on these little shoes like this.

798
00:40:58,100 --> 00:41:00,360
It gives us some leeway when it gets hot and cold

799
00:41:00,460 --> 00:41:02,800
to slide back and forth.

800
00:41:02,900 --> 00:41:05,140
The steam is piped to giant turbines.

801
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:12,200
This power is then transferred to a generator.

802
00:41:12,300 --> 00:41:15,880
That generator is generating 50 megawatts of electricity

803
00:41:15,881 --> 00:41:19,820
right now and up to power 50,000 homes.

804
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:21,820
The electricity is then sent all over

805
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:25,020
Northern California.

806
00:41:25,120 --> 00:41:27,220
Then it's time for the water to pay another visit

807
00:41:27,320 --> 00:41:28,920
to the hot rocks.

808
00:41:29,020 --> 00:41:30,820
What you see behind me here is the power plant

809
00:41:30,920 --> 00:41:32,020
cooling tower.

810
00:41:32,120 --> 00:41:34,640
After the steam has expended its energy in the turbine,

811
00:41:34,740 --> 00:41:36,840
it's condensed and sent out here to the cooling tower

812
00:41:36,940 --> 00:41:39,240
to be cooled where it could be injected back into the ground

813
00:41:39,340 --> 00:41:40,780
to produce more steam.

814
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:42,740
What you see coming out of the top of the cooling tower

815
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:44,040
is not smoke.

816
00:41:44,140 --> 00:41:45,880
It's just pure water vapor that's being cooled through

817
00:41:45,881 --> 00:41:49,120
We have an endless supply of energy

818
00:41:49,220 --> 00:41:50,880
in which we can generate power.

819
00:41:50,980 --> 00:41:53,880
This is renewable energy that works around the clock.

820
00:41:53,980 --> 00:41:57,560
The geothermal power comes up naturally 24 hours a day,

821
00:41:57,660 --> 00:41:59,420
seven days a week.

822
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:02,100
Across the globe, many countries are looking

823
00:42:02,200 --> 00:42:05,540
to the heat of hot rocks for future energy needs.

824
00:42:05,640 --> 00:42:08,240
The potential for geothermal energy is huge.

825
00:42:08,340 --> 00:42:11,300
The Earth has an inexhaustible supply of energy.

826
00:42:11,400 --> 00:42:13,800
Worldwide, geothermal energy is produced

827
00:42:13,801 --> 00:42:17,100
is produced in about 20 different countries.

828
00:42:17,200 --> 00:42:19,500
In areas of the world where steam isn't as close

829
00:42:19,600 --> 00:42:22,040
to the surface as it is at the geysers,

830
00:42:22,140 --> 00:42:24,540
engineers are experimenting with a process called

831
00:42:24,640 --> 00:42:27,540
hot dry rock technology.

832
00:42:27,640 --> 00:42:30,040
In hot dry rock geothermal technology,

833
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:33,080
there's no steam locked up in the hot rock

834
00:42:33,180 --> 00:42:34,960
that exists down under the crust.

835
00:42:35,060 --> 00:42:36,660
So what engineers have tried to do

836
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:39,560
is drill down into that rock, and then

837
00:42:39,660 --> 00:42:42,160
taking whatever water source they happen to have available

838
00:42:42,161 --> 00:42:45,360
have available at the surface, pump it down into a well,

839
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:47,960
let it work its way out into the cracks and fissures

840
00:42:48,060 --> 00:42:51,100
in that hot dry rock, and then drill more wells

841
00:42:51,200 --> 00:42:54,880
around the perimeter and try to recover that water as steam

842
00:42:54,980 --> 00:42:56,980
to produce electricity.

843
00:42:57,080 --> 00:43:00,340
The wells have to be deeper with hot dry rock technology.

844
00:43:00,440 --> 00:43:03,380
But theoretically, the process could produce enough energy

845
00:43:03,480 --> 00:43:07,060
to supply the entire world's demands.

846
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:10,660
Not bad for a bunch of rocks.

847
00:43:10,661 --> 00:43:13,160
Whether they're creating energy for our homes,

848
00:43:13,260 --> 00:43:15,300
iron for our industries, or concrete

849
00:43:15,400 --> 00:43:18,660
for our infrastructure, ROCK's partner with us

850
00:43:18,760 --> 00:43:21,100
in stony silence.

851
00:43:21,200 --> 00:43:25,800
They've stood by us in the past, and they will support our future.

852
00:43:25,900 --> 00:43:27,820
ROCK ON.

